 Here we have $x+y+z$ and $xy+xz+yz.$  So we start off using Vieta's, which says we want a cubic where the sum of i-th roots is $5$ and the sum of the product of the roots taken $2$ at a time is $8.$  So we want $(x-r)(x-s)(x-t)$ where $r,$ $s,$ and $t$ are some numbers that satisfy the above conditions.  Expanding out, we find that if the constant is $K,$ then
\begin{align*}
  x^3 & - (r + s + t)x^2 + (rs + st + tr)x - K \\
\end{align*}
Since the roots are $x,$ $y,$ and $z,$ we know $r + s + t = (x+y+z) = 5$ and $rs + st + tr = xy + xz + yz = 8.$  So we want
\begin{align*}
  x^3 - 5x^2 + 8x - K
\end{align*}
From here, we take inspiration and use our casework weights; as we want the largest value of $x,$ the casework weights are going to increase the power of $x$ the least but also make the polynomial factor nicely.  We notice that $x^3/5 + 8x/2 - Kx/10.$  We add in the casework weights, we see that
\begin{align*}
  \frac{x}{5} (x^2 + 8x + 20) = \frac{x}{5} (x+10)(x-2)
\end{align*}
We try to make $Kx / 10$ the first term on the right.  This suggests that $K = 5$ and $r = 10,$ $s = -2,$ and $t = -2.$  So we have
\begin{align*}
  (x-10)(x+2)(x+2) = x^3 - 5x^2 + 8x - 5
\end{align*}
So the answer is $10.$ </think> <answer> 10 </answer>

